#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:0
msgid ""
"Comprehensive glossary of snap and snapd terminology, including definitions "
"for common technical terms and concepts used in snapcraft."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:8
msgid "Glossary"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:10
msgid ""
"There are a significant number of terms and definitions that are unique to "
"the snap, snapd, and snapcraft ecosystem. This page defines the terminology "
"and other terms touched by these tools and links to further information when "
"required."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:12
msgid ""
"If you're new to using snaps, take a look at {ref}`Getting started "
"<tutorials-get-started>`, and if you're looking to build your own snaps, "
"take a look at [Craft a snap](https://documentation.ubuntu.com/snapcraft/"
"stable/tutorials/craft-a-snap/)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:14
msgid "Terms and definitions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:16
msgid "appliance"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:18
msgid ""
"An appliance is a pre-configured {ref}`Ubuntu Core <ref-glossary_ubuntu-"
"core>` bootable image that includes one or more snaps to provide a specific "
"set of features. The [OpenHAB](https://ubuntu.com/appliance/openhab) smart "
"home system, the [Plex](https://ubuntu.com/appliance/plex) media server, and "
"the [Nextcloud Server](https://ubuntu.com/appliance/nextcloud) platform, are "
"all available as appliances, for example."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:20
msgid ""
"See [What is an Ubuntu Appliance](https://ubuntu.com/appliance/about) for "
"more details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:22
msgid "assertion"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:24
msgid ""
"An assertion is a digitally signed document that either verifies the "
"validity of a process, as attested by the signer, or carries policy "
"information, as formulated by the signer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:26
msgid ""
"Snapcraft, snapd, Ubuntu Core and the Snap Store all use assertions to "
"handle a variety of functions and processes, including authentication, "
"policy setting, identification and validation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:28
msgid ""
"See {ref}`Assertions <explanation-security-assertions>` for more details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:30
msgid "base"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:32
msgid ""
"A  base is a special kind of snap that provides a run-time environment with "
"a minimal set of libraries that are common to most applications. They’re "
"transparent to users, but they need to be considered, and specified, when "
"building a snap."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:34 ../../../reference/glossary.md:69
#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:75 ../../../reference/glossary.md:81
#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:87
msgid ""
"See [Bases](https://documentation.ubuntu.com/snapcraft/stable/reference/"
"bases/) for more details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:36
msgid "branch"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:38
msgid ""
"A branch is an optional finer subdivision of a channel for a published snap "
"that allows for the creation of a short-lived sequences of snaps that can be "
"pushed on demand by snap developers to help with fixes or temporary "
"experimentation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:40
msgid ""
"See {ref}`Branches <ref-channels-and-tracks_branches>` for more details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:43
msgid "brand store"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:45
msgid "See {ref}`Dedicated Snap Store <ref-glossary_dedicated-snap-store>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:47
msgid "channel"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:49
msgid ""
"Channels define which release of a snap is installed and tracked for "
"updates. They consist of, and are subdivided by, tracks (`latest`, or "
"developer defined, e.g `1.0`), risk-levels (stable, candidate, beta and "
"edge), and optional branches. The _tracking_ value for an installed snap "
"shows which channel is being installed and followed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:51
msgid ""
"See {ref}`Channels <explanation-how-snaps-work-channels-and-tracks>` for "
"more details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:53
msgid "classic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:55
msgid ""
"_Classic_ is a snap confinement level that allows access to your system’s "
"resources in much the same way traditional packages do. It's used sparingly "
"and only after a manual review."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:57 ../../../reference/glossary.md:63
#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:102 ../../../reference/glossary.md:323
msgid ""
"See {ref}`Snap confinement <explanation-security-snap-confinement>` for more "
"details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:59
msgid "confinement"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:61
msgid ""
"A snap’s confinement level is the degree of isolation it has from your "
"system. There are three levels of snap confinement: strict, classic and "
"devmode. The majority of snaps use _strict_ confinement, and run in complete "
"isolation up to a level of minimal access that's always deemed safe, or "
"through access given via explicit interface connections."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:65
msgid "core"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:67
msgid ""
"_core_ is a base snap built from [Ubuntu 16.04 LTS](http://"
"releases.ubuntu.com/16.04/). It's different from _core16_ (see below) "
"because it bundles _snapd_ and its associated tools whereas core16 does not."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:71
msgid "core16"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:73
msgid ""
"_core16_ is a base snap built from [Ubuntu 16.04 LTS](http://"
"releases.ubuntu.com/16.04/). It's different from _core_ (see above) because "
"it does not include _snapd_ and its associated tools."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:77
msgid "core18"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:79
msgid ""
"_core18_ is a base snap built from [Ubuntu 18.04 LTS](http://"
"releases.ubuntu.com/18.04/). It's the former standard base for snap building."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:83
msgid "core20"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:85
msgid ""
"_core20_ is a base snap built from [Ubuntu 20.04 LTS (Focal Fossa)](https://"
"releases.ubuntu.com/20.04/), released April 23, 2020."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:90
msgid "Dedicated Snap Store"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:92
msgid ""
"A *Dedicated Snap Store* (formerly known as a *Brand Store* ) allows vendors "
"running Ubuntu Core and snap-based devices to control exactly what snaps are "
"available and when."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:94
msgid ""
"It can inherit selected packages from other snap stores, and host a set of "
"snaps specific to a brand and device models, and be either open to all "
"developers or a specific list."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:96
msgid ""
"See [Store overview](https://documentation.ubuntu.com/core/explanation/"
"stores/store-overview/) in our Ubuntu Core documentation for more details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:98
msgid "devmode"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:100
msgid ""
"_devmode_ is a snap confinement level used by snap developers when creating "
"their snaps. With *devmode*, a snap runs as a strictly confined snap with "
"full access to system resources, and produces debug output to identify "
"unspecified interfaces."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:104
msgid "epoch"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:106
msgid ""
"Epochs enable snap developers to control how users receive a new application "
"release when an application’s data format becomes incompatible with older "
"versions of the application."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:108
msgid ""
"When a new release breaks data compatibility with an older version, "
"incrementing the epoch in the new release stops old users automatically "
"refreshing to the new version."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:110
msgid ""
"See [Manage data compatibility](https://documentation.ubuntu.com/snapcraft/"
"stable/how-to/crafting/manage-data-compatibility/) in the _Snapcraft_ "
"documentation for more details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:112
msgid "extension"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:114
msgid ""
"Snapcraft extensions enable snap developers to easily incorporate a set of "
"common requirements into a snap. There are extensions to help with the "
"packaging of both Gnome and KDE Plasma applications."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:116
msgid ""
"See [Snapcraft extensions](https://documentation.ubuntu.com/snapcraft/stable/"
"reference/extensions/) for more details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:118
msgid "gadget"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:120
msgid ""
"A gadget is a device or other deployment running Ubuntu Core alongside a "
"vendor-specified, managed and maintained set of snaps. A gadget could be a "
"router, for example, a home automation device or even a VM cloud instance. "
"Its properties are defined within an embedded _gadget snap_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:122
msgid ""
"See {ref}`The gadget snap <reference-development-yaml-schemas-the-gadget-"
"snap>` for more details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:124
msgid "hook"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:126
msgid ""
"A hook is an executable that runs within a snap’s confined environment when "
"a certain action occurs. Actions include snap installation and removal, "
"changes to its configuration or connection state, and before or after a "
"refresh."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:128
msgid ""
"For more details, see {ref}`Supported snap hooks <reference-development-"
"supported-snap-hooks>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:130
msgid "interface"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:132
msgid ""
"An interface enables resources from one snap to be shared with another and "
"with the system. Interfaces require a connection, which is commonly made "
"automatically, or manually with the `snap connect` command."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:134
msgid ""
"For a snap to use an interface, its developer needs to have first defined "
"its corresponding plugs and slots within a snap’s [snapcraft.yaml](https://"
"documentation.ubuntu.com/snapcraft/stable/reference/project-file/snapcraft-"
"yaml) file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:136
msgid ""
"See {ref}`Interfaces <explanation-interfaces-all-about-interfaces>` and {ref}"
"`Interface management <how-to-guides-work-with-snaps-connect-interfaces>` "
"for more details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:138
msgid "Launchpad"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:140
msgid ""
"Launchpad is a code collaboration and secure build system for open source "
"projects. It is used by Ubuntu and other projects to coordinate work on bugs "
"and fixes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:142
msgid ""
"Launchpad provides the ability to build your snap for multiple architectures "
"- x86, ARM, RISC-V, POWER, s390. If you use Launchpad for snap building then "
"you need to provide it with your source code and snapcraft. It will build "
"and publish new revisions of your snap, which you can test and release. If "
"you do not already have a good multi-arch CI/CD system up and running then "
"we recommend you use Launchpad to support all devices with your snap."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:144
msgid ""
"See [Remote build](https://documentation.ubuntu.com/snapcraft/stable/"
"explanation/remote-build/) for more details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:146
msgid "layout"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:148
msgid ""
"Layouts help snap developers make snap-confined elements accessible from "
"locations such as  `/usr` ,  `/var`  and  `/etc` inside the snap. This helps "
"when using pre-compiled binaries and libraries that expect to find files and "
"directories outside of locations referenced by  `$SNAP`  or  `$SNAP_DATA`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:150
msgid ""
"They cannot be used to expose elements to non-permitted locations on the "
"host environment (such as exposing a file to `/etc/` on the host filesystem)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:152
msgid ""
"See [Snap layouts](https://documentation.ubuntu.com/snapcraft/stable/how-to/"
"crafting/use-layouts/) for more details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:154
msgid "LXD"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:156
msgid ""
"[LXD](https://linuxcontainers.org/lxd/introduction/) is a next generation "
"system container manager. It offers a user experience similar to virtual "
"machines but using Linux containers instead. It can be used by the "
"_snapcraft_ command to isolate the build process from the host system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:158
msgid ""
"See [Building with LXD](https://documentation.ubuntu.com/snapcraft/stable/"
"reference/build-environment-options/) for details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:160
msgid "Model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:162
msgid ""
"Snaps are a containerised application format which is designed for desktops "
"and devices. Unlike Docker images, which are designed for scale-out "
"environments where the mapping of hosts to containers can vary dynamically, "
"snaps are designed to be installed on a specific machine, alongside other "
"snaps. The snap container format allows for detailed integration between "
"snaps, using low-level host-specific capabilities like shared directories "
"and shared memory. These host-specific mechanisms are generally not used "
"with Docker, because one cannot predict if other containers will be on the "
"same machine or not."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:164
msgid ""
"Each machine where snaps are installed has its own sense of type - a model. "
"This comes from the IoT world, where a box which is acting as a security "
"camera recorder would be expected to have a very different software load "
"than a box which is acting as an elevator control system. The manufacturer "
"of the box specifies the model. Based on that model, snaps will follow "
"specific rules about software installation. For example, on an elevator "
"control system, the model might dictate that certain snaps must be "
"installed, and other snaps may not be installed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:166
msgid ""
"See the [Model assertion](https://documentation.ubuntu.com/core/reference/"
"assertions/model/) documentation for more details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:168
msgid "Multipass"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:170
msgid ""
"[Multipass](https://multipass.run/) is a lightweight VM manager for Linux, "
"Windows and macOS. It's designed for developers who want a fresh Ubuntu "
"environment with a single command. It uses KVM on Linux, Hyper-V on Windows "
"and HyperKit on macOS to run the VM with minimal overhead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:172
msgid ""
"By default, the  _snapcraft_ command uses Multipass to isolate the build "
"process from the host system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:174
msgid ""
"See [Set up Snapcraft](https://documentation.ubuntu.com/snapcraft/stable/how-"
"to/set-up-snapcraft/#install-multipass) for further details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:176
msgid "parallel installs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:178
msgid ""
"Parallel installs enable you to run multiple instances of the same snap on "
"the same system. Each instance is completely isolated from all other "
"instances, including its name, configuration, interface connections, data "
"locations, services, applications and aliases."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:180
msgid ""
"See {ref}`Parallel installs <interfaces-parallel-installs>` for more "
"information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:182
msgid "part"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:184
msgid ""
"A snap may seem like a single application but it can often include code from "
"many different open source upstream projects. The snapcraft build "
"description needs to specify, for each component, where to fetch it and how "
"to build it. We call each of those elements a *part*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:186
msgid ""
"Part definitions can be shared and reused, to enable many different snaps to "
"get the component without re-specifying in detail how to build it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:188
msgid "platform snap"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:190
msgid ""
"A platform snap contains the parts, packages, interface connections and "
"environment variables, among other elements, to enable other snaps to use a "
"platform without additional dependencies or configuration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:192
msgid ""
"Examples include kde-frameworks to provide KDE Plasma compatibility, and "
"WINE to help snaps more easily run Microsoft Windows executables."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:194
msgid ""
"A platform snap cannot be installed directly by users. They are instead "
"invoked by snap developers as the `default-provider` in a {ref}`content "
"interface <interfaces-content-interface>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:196
msgid "preseeding"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:198
msgid ""
"When Ubuntu Core boots for the first time, a seeding process installs an "
"initial set of snaps and runs their respective hooks."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:200
msgid ""
"_Preseeding_ speeds up this process by performing as many of these seed "
"administrative tasks as possible in advance when an image is created. During "
"deployment, snapd still performs the seeding process but it automatically "
"skips the parts that have already been performed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:202
msgid ""
"See [Preseeding](https://ubuntu.com/core/docs/preseeding) for more details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:204
msgid "refresh"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:206
msgid ""
"Snaps update automatically, and by default, the snapd daemon checks for "
"updates 4 times a day. Each update check is called a _refresh_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:208
msgid ""
"When, and how often, these updates occur can be modified with the snap "
"command. Updates can be set to occur on Friday at midnight, for example, or "
"for specific days of the month, such as only the third Monday, or even the "
"last Friday of the month, between 23:00 to 01:00 the next day."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:210
msgid ""
"See {ref}`How to manage updates <how-to-guides-work-with-snaps-manage-"
"updates>` for further details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:212
msgid "remote build"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:214
msgid ""
"Remote build is a feature in [Snapcraft](https://snapcraft.io/docs/snapcraft-"
"overview) (from  *[Snapcraft 3.9+](https://documentation.ubuntu.com/"
"snapcraft/stable/release-notes/)* onwards) that enables anyone to run a "
"multi-architecture snap build process on remote servers using [Launchpad]"
"(https://launchpad.net/). With remote build, you can build snaps for "
"hardware you don’t have access to and free up your local machine for other "
"tasks."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:216
msgid ""
"See [Remote build](https://documentation.ubuntu.com/snapcraft/stable/"
"explanation/remote-build/) for further details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:219
msgid "revision"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:221
msgid ""
"A snap's *revision* is a number assigned by the {ref}`Snap Store <ref-"
"glossary_snap-store>` automatically to give each snap a unique identity "
"within and across its channels."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:223
msgid ""
"It's important to note that there is no real concept of higher or lower snap "
"revisions and the current revision of the snap is simply the one that is "
"released onto a channel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:225
msgid ""
"The revision number is applied to the snap binary on upload to the Snap "
"Store, and while it does increment with each new upload, it is only used to "
"differentiate uploads."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:227
msgid ""
"The output to `snap info <snapname>` includes the revision for each snap in "
"each track and channel as a number in brackets after the publishing date:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:229
msgid ""
"channels:\n"
"  latest/stable:    20.0.7snap1               2021-02-05 (26119) 286MB -\n"
"  latest/candidate: ↑\n"
"  latest/beta:      20.0.7snap1+git11.5aeea85 2021-03-06 (26711) 284MB -\n"
"  latest/edge:      master-2021-03-09         2021-03-09 (26758) 292MB -\n"
"  20/stable:        20.0.7snap1               2021-02-05 (26119) 286MB -\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:238
msgid ""
"In the above example output, the latest/edge snap has a revision of `26758` "
"and is the most recent published revision of the snap."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:240
msgid ""
"However, neither the revision number (nor its version) enforce an order of "
"release. The local system will simply attempt to install whatever snap is "
"recommended by the publisher in the channel being tracked."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:242
msgid ""
"See {ref}`Revisions <explanation-how-snaps-work-revisions>` for further "
"details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:244
msgid "seeding"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:246
msgid ""
"When Ubuntu Core boots for the first time, the _seeding_ process installs an "
"initial set of snaps and runs their respective hooks."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:248
msgid ""
"Each installed snap needs to be verified and have their respective AppArmor "
"and seccomp security profiles, systemd units and mount points created. The "
"time this takes is proportional to the number of asserted snaps being seeded "
"but installing many snaps can impact first boot speed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:250
msgid ""
"The seeding process runs quicker with [preseeding](https://"
"documentation.ubuntu.com/core/how-to-guides/image-creation/optimise-boot-"
"speed/index.html)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:252
msgid "series"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:254
msgid ""
"In the domain of snaps, assertions and Ubuntu Core, the term _series_ is "
"used to indicate a version of backwards compatible snap namespaces and "
"assertion formats."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:256
msgid "This can most obviously be seen in the output to _snap version_:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:258
msgid ""
"$ snap version\n"
"snap    2.52\n"
"snapd   2.52\n"
"series  16\n"
"ubuntu  20.04\n"
"kernel  5.13.0-31-generic\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:267
msgid ""
"The above output shows that the installed package is compatible with other "
"`series: 16` snap assertions and namespaces."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:269
msgid ""
"A snap series **is not correlated** to an Ubuntu series, such as _18_ for "
"Ubuntu 18.04, or _20_ for Ubuntu 20.04, despite the numbers being the same "
"or similar. This similarity is due to initial design considerations that "
"have not yet been developed further, and the vast majority of snap series "
"definitions simply take the value of _16_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:271
msgid "snap"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:273
msgid ""
"Snaps are app packages for desktop, cloud and IoT that are easy to install, "
"secure, cross-platform and dependency-free, and _snap_ is both the command "
"line interface and the application package format. The command is used to "
"install and remove snaps and interact with the wider snap ecosystem."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:275 ../../../reference/glossary.md:356
msgid "See {ref}`Getting started <tutorials-get-started>` for more details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:277
msgid "snapcraft"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:279
msgid ""
"Snapcraft is both the command and the framework used to build your own "
"snaps. The command and framework are cross-platform and can help you to "
"easily build and publish your snaps to the [Snap Store](https://snapcraft.io/"
"store)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:281
msgid ""
"See [Craft a snap](https://documentation.ubuntu.com/snapcraft/stable/"
"tutorials/craft-a-snap/) for a tutorial on building a snap."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:284
msgid "snapd"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:286
msgid ""
"_snapd_ is the background service that manages and maintains your snaps."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:288
msgid ""
"Alongside its various service and management functions, snapd provides the "
"_snap_ command, implements the confinement policies that isolate snaps from "
"the base system and from each other, and governs the interfaces that allow "
"snaps to access specific system resources outside of their confinement."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:290
msgid ""
"See {ref}`Snap documentation <ref-index_snap-documentation>` for more "
"details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:292
msgid "snappy"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:294
msgid ""
"Snappy was the predecessor to Ubuntu Core. The term is still occasionally "
"used informally to refer to various aspects of the snap ecosystem, such as "
"the command, the package format, the Snap Store and Ubuntu Core. It's best "
"to avoid using this term; use *Snap* or *the Snap ecosystem* instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:296
msgid ""
"See {ref}`Snap documentation <ref-index_snap-documentation>` for general "
"details about the snap ecosystem."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:298
msgid "snapshot"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:300
msgid ""
"A  *snapshot*  is a copy of the user, system and configuration data stored "
"by  *snapd*  for one or more snaps on your system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:302
msgid ""
"Snapshots are generated manually with the  `snap save`  command and "
"automatically when a snap is removed. A snapshot can be used to backup the "
"state of your snaps, revert snaps to a previous state and to restore a fresh "
"snapd installation to a previously saved state."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:304
msgid ""
"See {ref}`Snapshots <how-to-guides-manage-snaps-create-data-snapshots>` for "
"further details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:307
msgid "Snap Store"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:309
msgid ""
"The Snap Store provides a place to upload your snaps, and for users to "
"browse and install. It hosts thousands of snaps for millions of users on "
"multiple architectures across 40 different Linux distributions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:311
msgid "See [snapcraft.io/store](https://snapcraft.io/store) for more details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:313
msgid "spread"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:315
msgid ""
"Spread is our open source testing utility that enables multiple shell "
"scripts to run in parallel on many different systems in an entirely "
"reproducible way. It currently runs a process that tests the snap ecosystem "
"on real-world platforms 150,000 times a day."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:317
msgid ""
"See [https://github.com/canonical/spread](https://github.com/canonical/"
"spread) for the project's code repository."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:319
msgid "strict"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:321
msgid ""
"_Strict_ is the default snap confinement level. It runs snaps in complete "
"isolation, and consequently, with no access your files, network, processes "
"or any other system resource without requesting specific access via an "
"interface."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:325
msgid "tracks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:327
msgid ""
"Tracks enable snap developers to publish multiple supported releases of "
"their application under the same snap name. They are one of the levels of "
"channel subdivision."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:329
msgid ""
"See {ref}`Tracks <explanation-how-snaps-work-channels-and-tracks>` for more "
"details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:331
msgid "Transitional interface"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:333
msgid ""
"A _transitional interface_ is an {ref}`interface <explanation-interfaces-all-"
"about-interfaces>` that can be used by a trusted snap to access traditional "
"Linux desktop environments that were not designed to integrate with {ref}"
"`snap confinement <explanation-security-snap-confinement>`. These interfaces "
"will become deprecated as replacement or modified technologies that enforce "
"strong application isolation become available."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:336
msgid "Ubuntu Core"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:338
msgid ""
"Ubuntu Core is Ubuntu for embedded devices and built using snaps. The "
"operating system is read-only, and updates are transactional, with an "
"absolute emphasis on maintaining a system’s integrity."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:340
msgid ""
"See our [Ubuntu Core](https://ubuntu.com/core/docs) documentation for more "
"details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:342
msgid "Version"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:344
msgid ""
"The *version* of a snap is a string assigned to a project by its developers. "
"You can see the version string assigned to a snap in the output from `snap "
"info <snapname>` or `snap find`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:346
msgid ""
"$ snap find nextcloud\n"
"Name          Version       Publisher   Notes  Summary\n"
"nextcloud     20.0.7snap1   nextcloud✓  -      A safe home for all your "
"data\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:352
msgid ""
"The version string typically reflects the general release version of a "
"snap's primary application, but it can equally be any arbitrary value "
"assigned by the snap creator."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/glossary.md:354
msgid ""
"The version string for the [Nextcloud snap](https://snapcraft.io/nextcloud) "
"in its latest/stable channel, for example, tracks the version of the latest "
"stable release, such as `20.0.7`. The version string for Nextcloud in its "
"latest/edge channel represents its source code branch and build date, such "
"as `master-2021-03-09`."
msgstr ""
